WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:11.850
- What kind of questions can
science not currently answer,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:12.843
but might soon?

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.800
When you think about the
problems and the mysteries

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.790
before us that may be
within reach of science?

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.260
- I think an obvious one
is the origin of life.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.860
We don't know how that happened.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.260
There's a difficulty in knowing
how it happen historically,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.940
actually literally on
Earth, but starting life

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.440
from non-life is something

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.650
I kinda think we're close to, right?

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.115
We're really--
- You really think so?

00:00:37.115 --> 00:00:38.300
- I do, I do.
- How difficult is it

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.700
to start life?

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.160
- Well, I've talked to people,
including on the podcast,

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.230
about this, life requires three things,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.140
life as we know it.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.440
So there's a difference between life,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.130
which who knows what it is,

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.940
and life as we know it,
which we can talk about

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.740
with some intelligence.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.780
So life as we know it
requires compartmentalization,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.590
you need a little
membrane around your cell,

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.910
metabolism, you take in food and eat it,

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.990
and let that make you do things,

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.230
and then replication.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.850
Okay, so you need to have some information

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.820
about who you are that you pass
down to future generations.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.720
In the lab, compartmentalization
seems pretty easy,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.260
not hard to make lipid bilayers that come

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.700
into little cellular walls pretty easily.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.203
Metabolism and replication are hard,

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.870
but replication we're close to.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.313
People have made RNA-like
molecules in the lab that,

00:01:29.902 --> 00:01:32.360
I think the state of the
art is they're not able

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.010
to make one molecule
that reproduces itself,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.540
but they're able to make two molecules

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.528
that reproduce each other.

00:01:37.528 --> 00:01:39.020
- (laughs) Yeah.
- So that's okay.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.020
That's pretty close.
- Yep.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.000
- Metabolism is harder, believe it or not,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.850
even though it's sort of
the most obvious thing,

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.890
but you want some sort
of controlled metabolism.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.450
And the actual cellular
machinery in our bodies

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.650
is quite complicated,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.930
it's hard to see it just
popping into existence

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.710
all by itself, it probably took a while,

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.090
but we're making progress.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.220
In fact, I don't think we're spending

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.550
nearly enough money on it.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.760
If I were the NSF, I would
flood this area with money,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.190
'cause it would change
our view of the world

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:09.800
if we could actually make life in the lab

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.663
and understand how it was
made originally here on Earth.

